 By Neil Irwin and Courtenay Brown &middot; Oct 28, 2026 
 
 
 How much of the hype around AI-fueled productivity gains is already becoming a reality in the halls of Corporate America? The answer is murkier than you might think.

 More below, including what we've gleaned from big companies' earnings calls. Plus, what to expect from tomorrow morning's first reading on third-quarter GDP. Situational awareness: The Federal Reserve's interest rate decision is due out at 2pm ET, followed by a press conference from chairman Kevin Warsh at 2:30. No rate change is expected; Neil will be in the room.

 Today's newsletter, edited by Jeffrey Cane and copy edited by Katie Lewis, is 854 words, a 3-minute read. 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: AI may not be boosting productivity much yet 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Federal Reserve Bank of San Francisco ; Chart: Courtenay Brown/Axios America is in the midst of its strongest productivity surge in decades. The evidence is more ambiguous about how much AI is the reason. 

 Why it matters: Companies are pouring hundreds of billions of dollars into AI, a massive bet that the technology will deliver sweeping productivity gains across the economy.

 But so far, the productivity gains may have more to do with less sexy developments, including the more intensive use of capital equipment and a post-pandemic surge in entrepreneurship now paying dividends. What they're saying: "Translating individual productivity to organizational productivity — there's a lot of slip between cup and lip," Keith Waddell, CEO of staffing firm Robert Half, told investors last week.

 "We're not ignoring that there are productivity gains for our people, and we're getting some," he added. S&amp;P Global aims for roughly 2% in annual productivity gains, but it cannot isolate AI's contribution from other operational changes.

 "Some of that is directly AI. Some of that is non-AI. Most of it is actually mixed and integrated," CFO Eric Aboaf said yesterday on the firm's earnings call. Between the lines: Increased investment and more intensive use of labor and equipment have contributed 1.3 percentage points to annual productivity growth since early 2023, according to the San Francisco Fed — nearly twice their contribution over the preceding two decades. 

 A broader measure of efficiency that strips out the effects of additional investment and more intensive use of resources has grown by 0.7% annually, compared with 0.5% from 2005 to 2022. Some of the investment may itself be AI-related, such as software that helps employees accomplish more in less time. In the Fed's Beige Book, San Francisco district businesses credited AI and traditional improvements, including training and streamlined processes, roughly equally for productivity gains. The other side: The productivity gains are more apparent among companies developing AI.

 Anthropic disclosed in its IPO filing this week that Claude wrote more than 90% of contributions to its codebase last month. The average daily volume of code contributed per engineer was more than 45 times its 2024 level, according to the prospectus. What to watch: Whether AI's benefits spread beyond tech giants and show up in broader corporate financial results.

 Revenue per employee and profit margins have climbed among the Magnificent Seven companies but remained broadly flat across the other 493 companies in the S&amp;P 500, Apollo chief economist Torsten Slok wrote over the weekend . "[T]he AI story will be decided in the S&amp;P 493, not the Mag 7," Slok wrote. 
 
